544
03:15:52,950 --> 03:16:37,780
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Atty. Dela Fuente, let me direct your attention to Section 8A of the AMLA. You already mentioned this a while ago but I'll read it for the benefit of the Senator, Judges, and the public. Information security and confidentiality states that, and I quote, the AMLC and its secretariat shall securely protect information received or processed and shall not reveal in any manner any information known to them by reason of their office. This prohibition shall apply even after their separation from the AMLC. You were part of the AMLC Secretariat. Please describe the powers and limitations of this office in terms of revealing confidential information.

545
03:16:38,460 --> 03:17:20,980
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Well, Your Honor, this is one of the, there are three rules on confidentiality and this is the third rule found on the first paragraph of 8-A. Now, it admits of an exemption on the second paragraph that is for authorized disclosure if this pursuant to information exchange. and if it is pursuant to an order of a court of competent jurisdiction. Now, again, the rule is that there is confidentiality should be the followed exception, again, if it is pursuant to information exchange and also pursuant to an order of a lawful court.

546
03:17:21,670 --> 03:17:28,420
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. What types of data? and information are considered confidential information under this provision.

547
03:17:28,880 --> 03:17:42,160
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: So confidential information here would cover the transaction reports, CTRs and STRs, derivatives of these CTRs and STRs. So that would be the intelligence reports and the investigation reports.

548
03:17:42,720 --> 03:17:47,840
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: And to be sure, how strict must the AMLC be with the rule on confidentiality?

549
03:17:48,080 --> 03:17:52,600
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: So again, under the same section, second paragraph,

550
03:17:56,230 --> 03:18:18,110
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Paragraph allowing for the disclosure. It says there that the AMLC shall formulate rules concerning information security, confidentiality, procedures for the proper handling, protection, storage of the information. So there are still safeguards. It is authorized but controlled disclosure.

551
03:18:20,030 --> 03:18:50,260
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Pertinent AMLC reports in this case contain a portion indicating general principles of information exchange with regard to the end user of confidential information from the AMLC. We don't want to flash the document on the screen, so I'll just go through the points one by one. We'll just get your comments thereon. Number one is, what does it mean? that the end user must first verify the information provided.

552
03:18:51,640 --> 03:19:17,290
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Okay, again, this is... Again, this is still subject to the verification of the end user. So, again, the report does not contain conclusions but rather intelligence information. So, again, it is up to the end user as to how they will appreciate the information.

553
03:19:17,720 --> 03:19:25,500
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, so in your experience po as a financial investigator, paano po ba ginagawa yung tamang pag-verify ng information contained in these reports?

554
03:19:26,180 --> 03:20:04,300
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Well again, the end user of the report, normally it's a law enforcement agency or an agency with investigative powers. There are things that are outside the... The view of AMLC. Again, money laundering lang sila. But dun sa unlawful activity, it's within the powers of the investigating unit, the law enforcement agencies. So again, some information might not have been disclosed sa AMLC. So it's up to the end user to verify if this information is useful or not.

555
03:20:05,320 --> 03:20:10,620
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Number two, what does it mean that the end user must protect the confidentiality of the information?

556
03:20:11,540 --> 03:20:39,390
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Again, since this information are, again, subject to verification, if it falls in the hands of, again, an unauthorized individual or organization, it might be used. It may deviate from its intended use or purpose. So we have to protect its confidentiality. It's subject, again, to verification. Subject to further checking by the end user.

557
03:20:39,850 --> 03:20:46,650
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Ano po ba yung tamang measures para masiguradong mapoprotektahan yung confidentiality ng mga impormasyon?

558
03:20:47,620 --> 03:21:08,050
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Number one po, ano? Depende po yan sa ilalagay ni AMLA. Kasi there are, again, different information that should be handled. Wala pong isang rule lang for every information. So normally, It should be restricted to the end user. The use should only be confined to the intended use indicated in the report.

559
03:21:08,470 --> 03:21:16,650
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, and proceed po tayo sa number 3. What does it mean that the end user must use the information for intelligence purposes only?

560
03:21:17,610 --> 03:21:23,890
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Okay, when we say for intelligence purpose only, it is for analytical purpose. Not

561
03:21:23,890 --> 03:21:35,410
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: for evidence gathering or leading to coercive measures. For case build-up, for information gathering.

562
03:21:36,100 --> 03:21:42,360
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. How proper po na i-disclose yung mga datos sa publiko kung wala naman pong court order?

563
03:21:43,950 --> 03:21:54,810
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Very dangerous po ito if we let this out in the public. Why? Because we are in fact compromising the safety and security of the concerned account holder, the

564
03:21:54,970 --> 03:22:00,250
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: covered person, and also the one who processed the information.

565
03:22:00,810 --> 03:22:10,010
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Thank you. Number four, last two na lang po. What does it mean that the end user must not use information as evidence in formal proceedings?

566
03:22:10,630 --> 03:22:11,970
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: For number four,

567
03:22:12,050 --> 03:22:13,870
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Your Honor, again,

568
03:22:14,450 --> 03:22:36,720
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: it is not the evidence. It is not the... Evidence itself. Yan ho ay for intelligence use only. Para ho siyang nagbibigay ng clues, investigative leads. It doesn't give you the exact evidence but it leads you. Tinuturo niya kung saan ang mga ebidensya. Leads

569
03:22:36,720 --> 03:22:39,040
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: lang ho ito. Parang clues. Nagbibigay ho ng clues.

570
03:22:39,780 --> 03:22:43,300
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Ano po ba yung mga formal proceedings contemplated under this rule?

571
03:22:43,940 --> 03:23:04,830
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: When we say my own appreciation po of formal proceedings, that would These are coercive proceedings, court proceedings, one that would involve appreciation of the facts and also a ruling, an order from a court of competent jurisdiction.

572
03:23:05,390 --> 03:23:17,590
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, and finally, what does it mean that the end-user must refrain from reproducing, copying, extracting, printing, or distributing material to any third party without the written consent of the council members?

573
03:23:18,950 --> 03:23:42,340
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: So I think your honor, it's self-explanatory. No forward dissemination. No re-dissemination from its intended user cannot go beyond those who are originally the intended end user. So it cannot be re-forwarded, it cannot be re-disseminated.

574
03:23:42,560 --> 03:24:00,570
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Now, my next question is this. What is the legal effect? Confidential data and information on VP Sara and Attorney Manz being disclosed in a public proceeding similar to what happened in the House Committee on Justice on April 22, 2026.

575
03:24:01,150 --> 03:24:12,070
Rep. Jose Manuel “Chel” I. Diokno: Your Honor, objection. The question is misleading. The documents produced by the AMLA before the Committee on Justice were based on a subpoena issued by the Committee. That should be clear.

576
03:24:13,410 --> 03:24:15,930
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: Subject to that qualification. Witness may answer.

577
03:24:18,130 --> 03:24:42,910
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: I'll repeat, ano pong legal effect kapag ang confidential data or information po ni VP Sara and Attorney Mans were disclosed in a public proceeding similar to what happened in the House Committee on Justice on April 22, 2026, subject po dun sa qualification na sinabi ni Representative Diokno, na sinabi na po yun, but with the added qualification na hindi po sila court.

578
03:24:43,500 --> 03:24:56,230
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Again, Your Honor, I have no personal knowledge as to the matter surrounding that scenario. But what I can relay, Your Honor, nasa Section 14D, if

579
03:24:56,230 --> 03:25:04,570
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: there is a violation of confidentiality, there is a corresponding criminal liability to those who violated that.

580
03:25:04,770 --> 03:25:16,830
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Now, just to ask another point on this, who will you consider po as end users? Of confidential information kapag ginawa na pong public yung hearing, for example.

581
03:25:18,750 --> 03:25:20,190
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Kapag ginawa ng public.

582
03:25:20,190 --> 03:25:28,810
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Kanyari, public na po yung hearing, since public po yung hearing and televised, who will you consider as the end users ng mga confidential information kapag gano'n?

583
03:25:29,560 --> 03:25:42,960
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Well, it doesn't convert the information from being confidential to now a unclassified information or a freely available information.

584
03:25:44,510 --> 03:25:51,050
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: The confidentiality nature of the information still remains with that data.

585
03:25:51,370 --> 03:26:08,090
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Now, based on your knowledge also as a financial investigator, ano po ba yung mga measures na can be used or observed when end-users receive confidential information in a public hearing, for example, like the one before the House Committee on Justice?

586
03:26:09,090 --> 03:26:32,420
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Your Honor, like the one flashed on the screen. A while ago, those are just some of the measures na nilalagay ko dun sa mga reports emanating from our AMLC. So that would restrict use of the information to only the intended users and will serve as a caution, as a reminder to those who might be accessing it without proper authority.

587
03:26:34,880 --> 03:26:56,800
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Let me direct your attention to section 14. You mentioned this, but I will no longer read. I will be more circumspect this time. But it talks about breach of information, security, and confidentiality. Itanong ko lang po sa inyo, as an expert on this matter, who are the responsible persons in case of breach of information, security, and confidentiality?

588
03:26:57,000 --> 03:27:02,200
Rep. Jose Manuel “Chel” I. Diokno: Objection, Your Honor. There's been no finding that this witness is an expert and that the subject of dispute.

589
03:27:02,600 --> 03:27:03,360
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: If he knows, Your Honor.

590
03:27:03,850 --> 03:27:10,730
Rep. Jose Manuel “Chel” I. Diokno: I object to the qualification of the witness or the statement describing him as an expert. I move to strike that portion.

591
03:27:11,690 --> 03:27:21,210
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: If he knows, counsel, in regard to the qualification or description as an expert, let it stay on the record, subject to cross with respect to his qualifications. Witness, may answer?

592
03:27:21,960 --> 03:28:03,900
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Your Honor, under Section 14D, as to who will be liable, again, the one, I think, the one here, po, no? Who access without authority The confidential information. So that's the first one. Second, if the offender is a public officer or employee, there is a corresponding accessory penalty. And third, if it is disseminated or disclosed by the mass media, and then it will also include the reporter, publisher, editor-in-chief, and as indicated under Section 14D.

593
03:28:03,900 --> 03:28:31,090
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: All right. Tuloy lang po natin sa paragraph F. Nakalagay doon imposition of administrative sanctions. Again, I won't read the entire provision but based on this provision po and based on your experience with AMLA also, what are the natures of the penalties involved po sa batas? Aside from those that you already mentioned.

594
03:28:32,540 --> 03:28:51,180
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Sa administrative sanctions mo under section 14, normally it includes monetary and non-monetary penalties. So it can range from a simple reprimand up to imposition of monetary penalties as high as 500,000 Philippine pesos per violation.

595
03:28:51,560 --> 03:29:06,890
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, so you mentioned media. So it will be a teaching moment for all of us as well. Based on your knowledge and experience as a financial investigator, how should the media handle confidential information as end users?

596
03:29:07,960 --> 03:29:28,650
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: As much as possible, it must be again in line with AMLA and our law on data privacy. Sensitive personal information should be redacted, removed, or blocked. So that it cannot reveal this information. It can be used weaponized against the account holder.

597
03:29:29,190 --> 03:29:55,500
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Let's go back to Section 7.1 of the AMLA. And I'll read, To investigate suspicious transactions and covert transactions deemed suspicious after determination by AMLC, money laundering activities, and other violations of this Act. So based on this provision, please describe to us. The process how the AMLC must conduct financial investigation based on CTRs and STRs. I-detail yun na

598
03:29:55,500 --> 03:29:57,660
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: po natin. Where does the investigation process begin?

599
03:29:58,400 --> 03:30:20,880
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Okay. When we say investigation, that’s already stage two. Prior to investigation, we have what we call financial intelligence. That’s stage one. So day-to-day, the AMLC receives, analyzes, and disseminates this financial intelligence information. So it can be again forwarded to law enforcement or its own financial investigation group.

600
03:30:21,340 --> 03:31:04,400
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: If there is already a predicate crime, saka na po siya pupunta sa second stage, financial investigation. The first one, stage one, financial intelligence is more on the analytical part. Analysis lang ho yan. Second part, that is the more coercive evidence gathering phase. That is the financial investigation stage. Now before mag-proceed ho sa ating mga coercive processes, there must be an AMLC, well, council authority. So that would pave the way for filing for a petition, for freeze order, bank inquiry. Civil Forfeiture. So, provided po siya ng Section 10, 11, and 12 ng ating AMLA.

601
03:31:04,580 --> 03:31:16,430
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. You mentioned yung referral to law enforcement agencies. Ano po ba yung mga measures that the AMLC observes in relation to this stage of the process?

602
03:31:18,280 --> 03:31:26,580
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Again, when the information, financial intelligence report is forwarded to law enforcement agencies, whether local or abroad,

603
03:31:28,780 --> 03:31:54,900
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: It's only used for intelligence purpose. Intelligence gathering or case build up. So normally po, it acts as a precaution or as a reminder dun sa end user that they have to verify, still verify, the information and if it will be helpful in their investigation. Kung makakatulong sa kanilang investigation at relevant pa yung information.

604
03:31:55,610 --> 03:31:58,970
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Thank you po. What documents ang ini-issue diyan po? Kung meron man.

605
03:32:00,240 --> 03:32:08,060
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Normally, your honor, again, the financial intelligence report or sometimes po, ano, na pag may financial investigation report ka.

606
03:32:08,220 --> 03:32:18,650
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: How about in relation to the law enforcement agency and the AMLA, meron po bang mga ini-execute the documents doon to protect the confidentiality?

607
03:32:18,850 --> 03:32:32,600
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Nandun na po yung mismo sa report itself. Naka-embed na siya. Naka-incorporate na po yung measures na yun sa dokumento para hindi na po sila magtingin-tingin pa or mag-refer sa iba pang dokumento.

608
03:32:33,620 --> 03:32:42,230
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Matanong ko lang po, kasama po ba yung sensitivity analysis sa methodology ninyo? Kung alam nyo lang po yung term. Sensitivity

609
03:32:42,230 --> 03:32:58,430
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: analysis? Well, wala po siya sa batas, wala po siya sa AMLA. It's an invest... Investigation technique. But it's not reflected po sa ating batas.

610
03:32:59,030 --> 03:33:23,150
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Sige po. Ako rin po hindi ko alam yung term na yun to be honest. First time ko po marinig yun dun sa so-called expert ng prosecution. And when asked about it, tungkol to yata sa mga bangkay na mainit-init pa. Yun yung explanation. Anyway, the court can just check the records if warranted. Pero dun po sa binanggit nyo na very detailed process. On the average, how long po yung proseso na to?

611
03:33:24,280 --> 03:33:46,600
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Your Honor, depende ho yan sa kaso. The number of subjects, the number of accounts, the periods with an S covered. Kasi marami ho yan, kukunin sa mga bangko, maraming records. So far as the timeline, duration of how far, how long will the investigation?

612
03:33:49,040 --> 03:33:55,520
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Mahaba ho. We have simple up to complex cases. So we don't have a specific timeline for that.

613
03:33:55,520 --> 03:34:16,720
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: All right, so mahirap pala pag two weeks lang. Anyway, let me direct your attention to paragraphs three, four and six of the same section. Let me read paragraph three: to institute civil forfeiture proceedings and all other remedial proceedings through the Office of the Solicitor General. Number four, to cause the filing of complaints with the

614
03:34:16,890 --> 03:34:50,130
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Department of Justice or the Ombudsman for the prosecution of money laundering offenses. And I'll jump to paragraph 6 to apply before the Court of Appeals. Ex parte for the freezing of any monetary instrument or property alleged to be laundered, proceeds from, or instrumentalities used in or intended for use in any unlawful activity as defined in Section 3I hereof. In line po dun sa... Pinaliwanag niyong pong proseso kanina. And kung hindi niyo lang po nadaanan pa, please explain to us the process involved under these paragraphs.

615
03:34:50,490 --> 03:35:06,480
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Well, in relation to Section 7, ibig sabihin lang po niyan, before we proceed to these coercive processes, there must be counsel, ng AMLC Council approval, resolution. So there must be authority po bago umabot sa ganyan mga proseso.

616
03:35:06,640 --> 03:35:28,690
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Now, yung CTRs po and STRs ni VP Sara and Attorney Mans, that were examined and processed were from 2006 to 2025. So merong doon na 20 years ago pa yung records. Itanong ko lang po, how does the concept of prescription apply sa mga ganong records, if at all po?

617
03:35:30,810 --> 03:35:59,190
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: In this case, Your Honor, wala kong prescription. As long as merong ongoing case, it can go as far back as 20 years ago. Ito ay nakakabuti rin, hindi lang sa pag-prosecute. Makakatulong din ito para maintindihan natin yung behavior ng account. Minsan nakakatulong ito para ma-explain yung unexplained, malaman ang mga unknown. So it can go both ways po.

618
03:35:59,430 --> 03:36:06,070
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, thank you po for that answer. Now, let me just ask, what is the burden of proof in money laundering cases?

619
03:36:08,040 --> 03:36:12,160
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Since it is criminal in nature, burden of proof is proof beyond reasonable doubt.

620
03:36:12,380 --> 03:36:13,440
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: And who has that burden?

621
03:36:14,580 --> 03:36:17,240
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Pagdating ho dyan ang ating prosecutors.

622
03:36:17,860 --> 03:36:34,360
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, very well. Now let's talk about the predicate crime or unlawful activity aspect real quick. How does the AMLC handle the predicate crime or unlawful activity aspect related to the CTRs and STRs reported to it?

623
03:36:35,360 --> 03:36:57,440
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Yung sa unlawful activity, Hindi yan obligation or responsibility ng AMLC. Yan ay nasa mga law enforcement agencies. Again, money laundering is a derivative offense. Doon lang siya sa stage 2, money laundering side. But unlawful activity, we leave it to the normal law enforcement and those that are into investigation.

624
03:36:58,160 --> 03:37:10,340
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Isang tanong na lang po regarding dito. How active or passive is the AMLC? In terms of prosecuting the predicate crime or unlawful activity aspect before the appropriate courts?

625
03:37:10,940 --> 03:37:21,620
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Again, pagdating mo sa predicate crime, it's outside the authority of the AMLC. They leave it to the law enforcement agency's prosecution side po.

626
03:37:21,820 --> 03:37:37,770
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Okay, thank you po. Dito po kasi sa impeachment, maraming mga prinesenta na CTRs and STRs. But let me ask you, what is the value? CTRs and STRs in terms of actually proving criminal activity?

627
03:37:39,730 --> 03:38:48,080
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: CTRs and STRs po, they are not the actual proof. They are indicators. It would invite scrutiny. In fact, marami ho minsan mga STRs, false positives. Ang purpose po nito, you're just giving AMLC a signal. Para ang nagbibigay kayo ng notice. So AMLC, ito sa mga covered persons na nagsasabit ng STRs and CTRs. Can you take a closer look at this specific STR? So they have to verify it. And then itong CTRs and STRs na ito, a single suspicious transaction report. Mahirap pong mapatunayan. You have to compile everything. Multiple CTRs, multiple STRs, multiple years. For you to have a clear picture. Para huyan nagbubuo kayo ng jigsaw puzzle. Isang puzzle ho, doesn't give you a clear picture or an idea about the entire picture. So you have to put all of these pieces together in order for you to have a clear understanding of what transpired and properly prosecute.

628
03:38:48,260 --> 03:39:03,200
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Thank you po. You mentioned the process of bank inquiries kanina. My question is, If further verification is necessary, what is the proper procedure of coordinating with banks or other covered persons?

629
03:39:04,240 --> 03:39:22,040
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Yan po, yung bank inquiry. It will open na yung mga debits, credits, the statement of accounts, the actual bank records. So it is, again, a compulsive process and before we can get that, it must be applied with the Court of Appeals, ex parte.

630
03:39:22,810 --> 03:39:39,240
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. And one last question on this, or at least two more. Portions po kasi of STRs were flashed on the screen. What is the propriety po of disclosing portions of STRs in a televised proceedings?

631
03:39:40,200 --> 03:39:53,910
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Again, Your Honor, as to the actual flashing, unfortunately, I have no personal knowledge how it was flashed or the circumstances surrounding it. I can only comment on what is reflected sa batas. Again, that would fall under the prohibition.

632
03:39:54,720 --> 03:40:02,140
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: So, just a teaching moment again, what alternative measures can be done so as not to compromise confidentiality in such a situation?

633
03:40:03,630 --> 03:40:15,910
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Well, so as not to compromise confidentiality, the rule, yun pong pamantayan na nakalagay dun sa report must be strictly observed. Yun lang po yun talaga, no?

634
03:40:16,010 --> 03:40:24,760
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright, sige. Thank you po. Now, let's... Now give the so-called expert some airtime and go to page 2 of his report. I'll read.

635
03:40:26,740 --> 03:42:39,740
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Fourthly, her unexplained wealth also comes from corporations that the spouses have ownership or business interests in. Substantial undeclared income of these corporations amount to fraud, which allows for the piercing of the veil of corporate fiction. Thus, the undeclared income of these corporations is attributable as direct income of the stockholders, which include the VP and her spouse. The declaration of her business interests in her SALN and information appearing in the General Information Sheet or GIS reflect the respondent's participation in various businesses. Now, two things I want to discuss with you. Atty. Dela Fuente. Number one, piercing the veil of corporate fiction and participation in business as per the SALN. Let's first talk about participation in business as per the SALN. Let me direct your attention to Article 7, Section 13 of the 1987 Constitution. Section 13. The President, Vice President, the members of the Cabinet, and their deputies or assistants shall not, unless otherwise provided in the Constitution, hold any other office or employment during their tenure. They shall not, during said tenure, directly or indirectly practice any other profession, participate in any business, or be financially interested in any contract with, or in any franchise or special privilege granted by the government, or any subdivision, agency, or instrumentality thereof. including government-owned or controlled corporations or their subsidiaries. They shall strictly avoid conflict of interest in the conduct of their office. So, Atty. Dela Fuente, marami po kasing public officer na may financial or business interests. Again, you may answer this based on the provision that we just read or in general. But what is your understanding po of conflict of interest?

636
03:42:40,510 --> 03:42:53,770
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Well, the performance... ng kanyang public office should not have any, whether, the business interest that he or she is holding should not benefit from it.

637
03:42:54,530 --> 03:43:42,520
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Now let me direct your attention to the salient portions of Volume 2 of the Record of Constitutional Commission, pages 402 to 403. This is according to Mr. Regalado. In the first place, we have stated that the purpose here is not only conflict of interest, but to enable them to give full-time attention to their duties. And then I'll skip to the next part. In other words, they can engage in any other revenue-producing, income-producing activity, legitimate in nature, provided it does not conflict with the objective sought in this section. So based on this portion of the deliberations, kindly explain legitimate income-producing activity by a public officer. If you know.

638
03:43:44,140 --> 03:43:47,000
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Income producing activity by a public officer.

639
03:43:47,240 --> 03:44:01,000
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Again, those that are derived from, again, from profession and from businesses that are, again, I think declared by that specific official. Ito po yung mga lehitimo na kanyang kinita.

640
03:44:01,160 --> 03:44:20,280
Atty. Mark C. Vinluan: Alright. Now, based on your knowledge po and experience as a financial investigator, let's just delve on this po. If the concerned individual Being investigated po by the AMLC, kunyari nag-invest up po kayo tapos public officer siya, what is the importance of studying that person sal and carefully?

