1685
07:14:02,640 --> 07:14:17,510
Sen. Francis “Kiko” Pangilinan: Yes, but the forensic expert accessed the information. Impeachment Court allowed it. So you're saying that the impeachment court, in your opinion, committed a violation of the anti-money laundering law?

1686
07:14:17,750 --> 07:14:24,350
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: No, your honor. The premise you allowed it, then it is already permitted.

1687
07:14:24,750 --> 07:14:45,160
Sen. Francis “Kiko” Pangilinan: So if we compel you to review, I mean hypothetically, since you like to review the report, the forensic report, and ask you to come back and testify as to What you know about the forensic report, then that would be legal.

1688
07:14:46,190 --> 07:14:59,810
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: If it's the order of the impeachment court, that would be legal. But again, under the law, kasi si AMLC lang ang sole agency that should be doing that job.

1689
07:15:00,110 --> 07:15:11,320
Sen. Francis “Kiko” Pangilinan: Well, with respect to unauthorized disclosures, but the AMLC... are allowed to provide, is allowed to provide information on authorized disclosures.

1690
07:15:11,360 --> 07:15:11,860
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Yes, Your Honor.

1691
07:15:12,000 --> 07:15:22,630
Sen. Francis “Kiko” Pangilinan: Okay. So, no, and therefore, impeachment court, when it commands or it subpoenas or it compels, that is an authorized disclosure.

1692
07:15:23,030 --> 07:15:23,550
Atty. Rainer P. Dela Fuente: Yes, Your Honor.

1693
07:15:23,730 --> 07:15:26,950
Sen. Francis “Kiko” Pangilinan: Okay, no other questions. Thank you. Thank you for that clarification, Attorney.

