07:22:46,730 --> 07:23:21,350
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: After the entire day of hearing and receiving the testimony of our first witness for today and for this week... We have observed that the court and members of this court as well as the prosecution are interested in the proper application of Article 7, Section 13. And so, since this court may take mandatory judicial notice of Supreme Court decisions as well as decisions of the Sandigan Bayan in criminal cases acquitting the accused charging.

1749
07:23:24,020 --> 07:23:42,380
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: Individuals with purported violations of this provision in relation to RA 6713 or the Code of Ethical Conduct for Public Officers and Employees. We would like to direct the attention of this Court to the following provisions of the law.

1750
07:23:44,100 --> 07:23:45,080
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: You may proceed.

1751
07:23:45,360 --> 07:23:46,220
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: Yes, Your Honor.

1752
07:23:48,140 --> 07:27:14,050
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: Generally, divestment and conflict of interest are defined under sections 3, paragraph I of Republic Act 6713. Divestment is defined under Section 3, paragraph K of the same law. And in relation to that, the implementing rules and regulations of Republic Act 6713 under Rule 7 provides for the contents of SAL and under Section 1, paragraph A, subparagraph. Subparagraph 1 and Section 1, Subparagraph A, Subparagraph 2 provides for a disclosure of business interests and financial connections in relation to the requirement under Section 13, Article 7 of the 1987 Constitution. Now, in relation to that also, we have Rule 9 of the Implementing Rules and Regulations of RA 6713 providing actually and explicitly providing For the elements of conflict of interest to arise and the necessity of divestment. In fact, this is provided under Section 1, Subparagraph B of Rule 9 of RA 6713, Implementing Rules and Regulations. And I will read just for the record and so that the public may also hear. Under Section 1, Subparagraph B, conflict of interest occurs When the official or employee is a substantial stockholder or a member of the board of directors or an officer of the corporation or an owner or has substantial interest in a business or a partner in a partnership and number two, the interest of such corporation or business or his rights or duties therein are opposed to or affected by the faithful performance of official duty. Now, with respect to that conflict of interest, the said implementing rules and regulations, meaning the law, provides that under Section 2, subparagraph B, divestment is mandatory only if all the conditions in Section 1, subparagraph B, All the elements enumerated under the preceding paragraph must first concur before the public official or employee may be required to divest himself of such financial or alleged financial interest in any firm or company. Now, I will be citing two Supreme Court decisions which form part of the law of the land. These are, the first one is Trieste. Sr. v. Sandigan Bayan, GR number L-70332-43. This was decided by the Supreme Court on November 13, 1986. And the Court said in that decision of Trieste that what is contemplated in Section 3, Paragraph H of the Antigraph Law is the actual intervention in the transaction. In which one has financial or pecuniary interests in order that liability may attach. And the official... May I just finish, Your Honor? I will also listen to the good prosecutor after I'm done.

1753
07:27:14,270 --> 07:27:14,870
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: The

1754
07:27:14,870 --> 07:27:15,330
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: official

1755
07:27:15,330 --> 07:27:16,490
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: need

1756
07:27:16,490 --> 07:27:16,690
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: not...

1757
07:27:16,690 --> 07:27:20,910
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: To continue.

1758
07:27:21,190 --> 07:27:57,080
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: The official need not dispose his shares in the corporation as long as he does not do... Anything for the firm in its contract with the office. For the law aims to prevent the use of influence, authority, and power. And the court cited the deliberations on Senate Bill 293, May 6, 1959. This is part of the Congressional Record, Volume 2, page 603, as well as page 604 of the Congressional Record, May 9, 1959. Now, more recently, in 2007,

1759
07:28:02,000 --> 07:28:59,940
Atty. Sheila C. Sison: The Supreme Court also rendered a decision in Caballero v. Sandigan Bayan. It reiterated the Trieste ruling that what is contemplated in the law is the actual intervention in the transaction in which one has financial or pecuniary interest in order that liability may attach. And finally, we also found a decision In People v. Dimaala of the Sandigan Bayan, where the prosecution witness was the presiding justice or chairperson of the 3rd Division that acquitted the accused in that case, criminal cases numbers 27, 208, 10, 12, 14, 16, 17, 19, 23, 24-28 in an amended decision dated June 22, 2018, Your Honor. That's the gist of my manifestation.

1760
07:28:59,960 --> 07:29:10,020
Sen. Francis "Chiz" G. Escudero: Court takes judicial notice of the law cited as well as the decision cited which form part of the law of the land. Yes, Honorable Ridon, you are entitled to your own manifestation.

